[TEXT] **Found it.** The arping test is definitive:

- `laptop0 → arping 172.16.10.13 (laptop3)`: **100% TIMEOUT** — 3/3 ARP broadcasts unanswered  
- `laptop3 → arping 172.16.10.12 (laptop2)`: **100% answered** — same-VLAN ARP works  
- `laptop0 → arping 172.16.20.1 (its own gateway)`: **100% answered** — VLAN 20 is functioning

This proves laptop0 and laptop3 (physically adjacent ports fa7 and fa4 on switch1) are in **completely different L2 broadcast domains**. They cannot ARP each other directly. File-sharing protocols (SMB/NetBIOS, mDNS) depend entirely on L2 broadcast discovery, so "nothing in either direction" is the result — even though IP routing via the gateway technically works.

