This proves laptop0 and laptop3 (physically adjacent ports fa7 and fa4 on switch1) are in **completely different L2 broadcast domains**. They cannot ARP each other directly. File-sharing protocols (SMB/NetBIOS, mDNS) depend entirely on L2 broadcast discovery, so "nothing in either direction" is the result — even though IP routing via the gateway technically works.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch1, laptop0
diagnosis_text:
The root cause is a VLAN misconfiguration on switch1: the access port serving laptop0 (eth7 / fa7) has been assigned to the wrong bridge domain, **br_vlan20**, while all other active office ports on the same switch (fa1–fa4, serving pc4, pc5, laptop2, laptop3) belong to **br_vlan10**. The "adjacent" laptops are laptop0 (fa7) and laptop3 (fa4), physically at neighboring desks but now in separate broadcast domains.

The mechanism is Layer-2 isolation: the Linux bridge on switch1 has two separate bridge instances — br_vlan10 and br_vlan20 — that do not exchange frames. ARP broadcasts from laptop0 (172.16.20.10/24, VLAN 20) never reach laptop3 (172.16.10.13/24, VLAN 10) and vice versa. Without ARP resolution, there is no direct L2 path, so all application-layer services that rely on broadcast discovery (SMB/NetBIOS, mDNS, Samba) fail completely — exactly the "nothing in either direction" the users describe. IP-layer routing via router0 still functions (TTL-decremented ICMP works), but file-sharing stacks require L2 reachability and cannot fall back to routed paths automatically.

Root cause: switch1.eth7 was placed into br_vlan20 (along with a matching IP reconfiguration on laptop0 to 172.16.20.10/24) instead of br_vlan10, splitting laptop0 off from its office segment.

Corrective fix: On switch1, detach eth7 from br_vlan20 and attach it to br_vlan10 (`ip link set eth7 nomaster && ip link set eth7 master br_vlan10`). On laptop0, change the IP from 172.16.20.10/24 to a 172.16.10.x address (e.g., 172.16.10.10/24) and update the default gateway to 172.16.10.1.
=== END ===